WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:02.820
Film Courage: I know we were having a conversation
before we got going [before the filming] about

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:07.720
the competition online and how hard it is
it is to get people to see your work.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:14.960
Where do you stand on creating things that
audiences may be willing to pay for versus

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.439
creating work that is meaningful to you and
maybe it’s going to [appeal] to a little

00:00:19.439 --> 00:00:20.439
niche audience?

00:00:20.439 --> 00:00:26.081
Jack Perez, Filmmaker: Well I don’t think
I’ve every been any good at let me just

00:00:26.081 --> 00:00:30.539
craft something just to be a big audience
favorite.

00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:35.899
Because I think that it’s hard enough for
the filmmaker (for me) to get interested in

00:00:35.899 --> 00:00:38.600
a particular story anyway.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:43.640
In other words I never felt that…even though
I can execute any story and I’ve done television

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:48.460
and I’ve done things that were scripted,
I can make that movie, I can make that hopefully

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.950
work.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:58.059
To set out to make a film, that story has
to be meaningful personally otherwise I won’t

00:00:58.059 --> 00:01:03.679
have the strength to go through all the obstacles
and the “no’s”…there has to be something

00:01:03.679 --> 00:01:07.750
driving that for me personally.

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:13.580
And I never thought I was necessarily skilled
as an architect of that kind of audience,

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:16.770
crowd-pleasing, super commercial movie.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:24.110
I just hope that because I do love commercial
cinema (there is plenty of commercial movies

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:32.060
that I love) that some how that love by way
will find its way into what I’m doing and

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:38.860
contain those elements and hopefully it will
be more personal.

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:47.750
But yes…I’d probably be a wealthy man
if I was skilled in that.

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:52.290
But I think the people who do make commercial
movies (we can rattle them off) going all

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:59.720
the way back to Spielberg or Michael Bay,
they are, I think that is how they think.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:04.530
In other words I don’t think they are sitting
around saying “Let’s make a commercial

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:05.530
movie.”

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:11.980
That may be on their mind but their taste
are exactly that commercial so it just happens

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:17.750
that the audience is of course going to connect
because they are that audience and they are

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:20.590
the perfect and they are the perfect people
to make that stuff.

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:25.340
Otherwise everybody would be able to make
that if it was simply a matter of instructions

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:28.720
on how to make…and then everybody would
then just go “Yeah, I want to be rich.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:29.950
Let’s just do that.”

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:34.540
And it’s not for lack of trying, everybody
tries, a lot of people try.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:42.740
But for me I have to find a story that is
valuable in some way to me and then I’m

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:49.650
happy to add on all of these “commercial
components” or exploitative components or

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:53.910
any of those things that you find in a commercial
movie but at least the foundation is personal.

00:02:53.910 --> 00:03:00.060
Film Courage: I’m thinking of a movie like
(and maybe people will disagree with me) but

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:03.640
INTO THE WILD about Christopher McCandless’s
story.

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:07.010
I hadn’t heard of the book [by author John
Krackauer] before the movie came out and did

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:09.790
not realize there was this whole cult following.

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:14.720
But that is one where you wouldn’t expect
the audience to resonate (even though I think

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:20.860
it’s a fantastic movie and I loved it) but
it’s a very special story and it’s not

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:26.460
about an everyday thing but some how it was
able to resonate with millions of people.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:34.340
Jack: Right, well probably because of the
very specific nature of the story about somebody

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:37.810
putting themselves out into the wilderness
into that extreme.

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:43.290
It’s not the specific act that everyone
can relate to, I think it’s the internal

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:45.090
thing that everyone is going through.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:51.630
That’s what I’m talking about, that there
is something universal in those feelings.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:55.630
I think for example that’s why they always
talk about…everybody involved with TAXI

00:03:55.630 --> 00:04:00.120
DRIVER said “Were making this for us but
probably no on is going to get it.”

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:06.849
And then it turned out to be this huge hit
because there was a billion very alienated,

00:04:06.849 --> 00:04:10.959
lonely, angry individuals out there that went
“Oh my God!

00:04:10.959 --> 00:04:13.190
I identify with that.”

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:21.139
So I think we’d be a lot richer filmicley
if the business allowed for more of those

00:04:21.139 --> 00:04:30.759
movies to be made because a producer is going
to be for something that obviously people

00:04:30.759 --> 00:04:32.639
can relate to.

00:04:32.639 --> 00:04:37.520
But people are much more complicated than
the way they are depicted in Hollywood movies.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:43.520
And so every once in a while when something
presents a reflection of that if it gets out

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:47.750
there I think it will be successful because
people will be like “Thank you!

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:53.439
Thank you for showing me that I am represented.”

00:04:53.439 --> 00:04:58.469
As opposed to it just being a cartoon or an
advertisement for life.

00:04:58.469 --> 00:05:04.559
Film Courage: So even something like LEGALLY
BLONDE and it’s a great film which I enjoyed

00:05:04.559 --> 00:05:11.229
but it makes light of someone who is ostracized
and in real life people probably wouldn’t

00:05:11.229 --> 00:05:14.860
be like “Well, I’m just going to get all
dolled up and go in there and show ‘em!”

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:20.610
It’s going to be a lot of pain behind the
scenes but it’s definitely showing something

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:24.800
that many people have been through but in
a light way where it feels safe.

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:32.289
Jack: Well that’s bound to be the more commercial…if
you can illustrate those really painful, awkward

00:05:32.289 --> 00:05:39.210
things in a way that manages to be entertaining
or safe (which I think is a really good word),

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:44.809
because most people go to the movies…they
go to the movies they take it like a tonic.

00:05:44.809 --> 00:05:49.689
They are not going to be…unless you are
going to a torture film, you’re not going

00:05:49.689 --> 00:05:52.770
to be ripped apart, you’re going to be soothed.

00:05:52.770 --> 00:06:01.259
So camlflouging or sugar-coating or I don’t
know how you put it, real things in a Hollywood

00:06:01.259 --> 00:06:08.949
way is a real art and a real trick and if
that can happen…like for me I resisted GROUNDHOG

00:06:08.949 --> 00:06:09.949
DAY.

00:06:09.949 --> 00:06:12.569
When GROUNDHOG DAY came out I was like “This
is just a one note thing.”

00:06:12.569 --> 00:06:16.539
I think most people thought that “Oh this
guy wakes up every day…hah hah?”

00:06:16.539 --> 00:06:20.819
And then when you actually watch the movie
Harold Ramus and Bill Murray do this brilliant

00:06:20.819 --> 00:06:29.409
job at getting at a lot of real deep things
in this very silly, gimmicky movie and that

00:06:29.409 --> 00:06:34.990
was obviously their intent that dealing with
somebody who is bitter and had given up and

00:06:34.990 --> 00:06:39.880
doesn’t give a sh*t about people anymore,
has disconnected and is just a user and then

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:46.909
from this twist of fate now has to go through
the stages of processing that until he becomes

00:06:46.909 --> 00:06:48.059
a human being.

00:06:48.059 --> 00:06:53.330
I mean if you made a straightforward movie
about that without the gimmick I don’t think

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:56.340
it would be as popular necessarily, people
wouldn’t go to see it.

00:06:56.340 --> 00:07:06.039
But with the twist and the comedy and the
Bill Murray aspect, the whole thing, it becomes

00:07:06.039 --> 00:07:14.029
this much more pleasurable thing to swallow
and a surprise and that’s why I love this

00:07:14.029 --> 00:07:15.189
movie because I thought Wow!

00:07:15.189 --> 00:07:19.819
It was this superficial, sh*tty thing and
it turns out to be actually quite complex

00:07:19.819 --> 00:07:22.620
interesting movie and I love that.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:29.129
And there are times when I want that or my
message or my identification in a nice candy

00:07:29.129 --> 00:07:32.349
package, I don’t always want it rough.

